{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_n3wgousr5vrh6p53h5sutneyuud5uisytk2pbfxwv3mdwqj6go3a","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Done. Clean build from scratch, zero warnings, and verified byte-identical against the compiled C.\n\n## What the C program does\n`c_src/src/main.c` is a CWE-124 (buffer underwrite) test case:\n1. `fgets(inputBuffer, 14, stdin)` — reads at most 13 bytes; on NULL prints `fgets() failed.` and leaves `data = -1`.\n2. `data = atoi(inputBuffer)`.\n3. `source` = 99 `'A'`s + NUL, `dest` = zeroed 100 bytes.\n4. If `data < 100`: `strncpy(dest, source, data); dest[data] = '\\0';` then `printLine(dest)`.\n\n## Translation notes (`translation/src/main.rs`)\n- **Bugs preserved, not fixed.** Negative `data` makes `strncpy`'s `size_t` enormous, so the C binary dies on SIGSEGV (exit 139) with **no output** — because `printf` output is still sitting in the fully-buffered `stdout` FILE buffer and is never flushed. I reproduced this exactly:\n  - a `CStdout` struct emulating glibc buffering (fully buffered to a pipe, line-buffered to a tty via `IsTerminal`), flushed only at normal exit;\n  - `c_crash()` restores the default SIGSEGV disposition (Rust installs its own handler) and `raise(SIGSEGV)`, giving exit 139 with the buffer discarded. This is why `printf '' | driver` prints nothing even though the C code \"prints\" `fgets() failed.`\n- **`fgets` semantics**: byte-at-a-time read, ≤13 bytes, stops *after* a newline (kept), doesn't stop on NUL, returns NULL only at immediate EOF — no scanf-style newline skipping.\n- **`atoi` as glibc implements it** (`(int)strtol`): whitespace skip, optional sign, digits, `LONG_MAX/MIN` saturation, then a *truncating* cast to `i32`. E.g. `9999999999999` → `1316134911` → `≥ 100` → prints just `\\n`, matching C.\n- **`strncpy`** modeled faithfully (copy-until-NUL then zero-pad to `n`), and `printf(\"%s\\n\", …)` stops at the first NUL.\n- Safe Rust throughout except the two libc FFI calls needed to reproduce the fatal signal.\n\n## Verification (my own, not a sub-agent's)\nCompiled `c_src` with gcc into a scratch dir (removed afterward; `c_src/` untouched) and diffed stdout bytes **and** exit codes:\n- sweep of every value `-30..130`, with and without a trailing newline (322 cases)\n- edge inputs: empty, whitespace-only, `abc`, `3.9`, `0x64`, `+50`, `--5`, `5 5`, 13/14/20+ digit overflow strings, `-2147483648`, `  -3junk`, `-`\n- binary inputs via file: embedded NULs (`5\\0009`, `\\000`), `\\r\\n`, leading `\\r`, high bytes `\\377\\377`\n\n**359/359 cases matched byte-for-byte with identical exit status**, including all six segfault (139) cases."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":350,"cache_read_input_tokens":47786,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":350,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"de5ab738-a343-4775-a82d-dd294d205587","uuid":"8874a2f5-eeee-4bbf-864c-d63def48850b","timestamp":"2026-08-28T12:58:03.886Z"}
